[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d ", &time_stamp)` on input such as `-x` reads the `-`, then finds a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-digit and reports a matching failure. glibc pushes back only the *one*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m offending character (`x`); the sign and the whitespace already skipped for the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m conversion stay consumed. The Rust `scan_int_then_space` was rewinding the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stream to the position it held before the conversion started, so the `-` was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m still available and was later picked up by `%80[^\n]` as part of the comment.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `Scanner::scan_int_then_space` no longer restores `self.pos` on a matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure: it returns `0` leaving the sign consumed and the offending character
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unread, which is exactly one character of pushback.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
